Z||000002||Old Lar||02-02-2001||04:07 PM||||I haven't done the repair, but read about it.  Helps a lot to have two people.  Door is heavy and you need a jack or something to rest the door on, once you pull the pins.  I'll search through several years of articles to see if I can locate it, once I get home.||||reg||11
Z||000003||fiero56||02-02-2001||04:29 PM||||WTIII did the drivers door on his the other week. With help, it shouldn't take any more than 5 minutes(it took him less time than that, probably about 2, and that includes searching for a hammer and screwdriver).||||reg||1
Z||000004||CHARLIE FERGUSON||02-02-2001||04:31 PM||||I've just replaced the top hingepin and bushings on the drivers door. it was real easy. i used a small tack hammer to tap the old pin out, then pryed the bushings out with a screw driver. (while using a floor jack to support the door.)i then tapped the new bushings in using a hex bolt to tap them<BR>in, then tap the new pin in place.by the way pontiac does not supply new pins, but the pins for a '90 s-10 will work. i am still figuring out how to start the lowers.||||reg||3
Z||000005||sqoach||02-02-2001||06:11 PM||||damn, this is a pain in the ass. the bottom pin looks like it won't be able to come out because of that spring in the way. how did you bypass this???||||reg||8
Z||000006||daved||02-02-2001||06:29 PM||||Not taking credit for this...someone else on here posted this a while ago....<P>Sagging doors can be fixed by replacing the door hinge pins and<BR>&gt; bushings. Chevrolet S-10 (Small truck)hinge pins and bushings are readily<BR>&gt; available at your local dealer, are inexpensive, are a perfect fit, and can<BR>&gt; be installed without removing door skins. I have replaced the drivers door<BR>&gt; hinge pins and bushings on both my 88's with S-10 parts. Simply support<BR>&gt; the back edge of the door with wooden blocks and rags so you don't scratch<BR>&gt; it and the old pins can be removed using various tools ( small pry bar,<BR>&gt; screwdriver and small hammer, etc). Yes they are somewhat difficult to get<BR>&gt; to, but I assure you it can be done (both hinges) in about an hours time.<BR>&gt; I recommend replacing both hinge pins and all bushings at once. If I<BR>&gt; recall correctly, it cost about $12 to do this. I have removed both hinge<BR>&gt; pins at the same time in this manner without having to completely remove<BR>&gt; the door. Just be careful to keep the door from falling over or rubbing on<BR>&gt; paint. I stuffed rags around edges to protect the door and rocker panel<BR>&gt; and slightly raised the rear edge of the door with a floor jack before<BR>&gt; putting wooden blocks under it for support.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||02-02-2001||07:26 PM||||notes in my cave. the spring has to come out. there is a tool for it. you may be able to borow one from a parts store. some of the chain stores have loaners.||||reg||1
